We can see both for .onion and non-onion traffic huge performance penalties in the mentioned past DoS attacks. Compared to that the performance impact this time is negligible.

Secondly, in the past we saw huge spikes in onion services traffic during big DoS waves. However, that's not the case this time. Instead it seems slow unusual *drops* (see the marked area at the end of the graph below) are visible:

![hidserv-rend-v3-relayed-cells-2022-06-18-2026-09-16](/uploads/578fc7adac9b7ee3ff6f346814498841/hidserv-rend-v3-relayed-cells-2022-06-18-2026-09-16.png){width=576 height=360}

We have evidence that there are onion services targeted this time (again) and evidence as well that enabling [PoW](https://blog.torproject.org/introducing-proof-of-work-defense-for-onion-services/) helps in the current situation. So, onion service operators affected by this DoS should try to use that defense if possible.

