When client authorization is configured, `hs_config_client_auth_all()` checks the private directory, enumerates its files, and calls `get_creds_from_client_auth_filename()` for each one (`src/feature/hs/hs_client.c`, lines 2493-2526 in Tor 0.4.9.11). The loader reads the whole file and passes it to the parser:

```c
client_key_str = read_file_to_str(client_key_file_path, 0, NULL);
if (!client_key_str) {
 log_warn(LD_REND, "The file %s cannot be read.", filename);
 goto err;
}

auth = parse_auth_file_content(client_key_str);
```

In `parse_auth_file_content()`, the fourth field is assigned directly to `seckey_b32`. The length check then interpolates that field into a WARN message:

```c
seckey_b32 = smartlist_get(fields, 3);

if (strlen(seckey_b32) != BASE32_NOPAD_LEN(CURVE25519_SECKEY_LEN)) {
 log_warn(LD_REND, "Client authorization encoded base32 private key "
 "length is invalid: %s", seckey_b32);
 goto err;
}
```

This is the decisive failure. The parser correctly rejects the malformed key, but it sends the attacker-relevant secret field to the logging sink without calling `safe_str_client_opts()`, `safe_str_opts()`, or otherwise redacting it. Safe logging is implemented through those helpers rather than by automatically rewriting every `log_warn()` argument, which the offline run confirms.

The same function contains a second, source-confirmed instance of the same root cause for a full-length string that fails Base32 decoding:

```c
if (base32_decode((char *) auth->enc_seckey.secret_key,
 sizeof(auth->enc_seckey.secret_key),
 seckey_b32, strlen(seckey_b32)) !=
 sizeof(auth->enc_seckey.secret_key)) {
 log_warn(LD_REND, "Client authorization encoded base32 private key "
 "can't be decoded: %s", seckey_b32);
 goto err;
}
```

